        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d2a8d4e2-415f-5446-826a-9652f01f509d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Ferrari 360, Ferrari F355, Ferrari F430, Porsche 911, Lamborghini Diablo, Mitsubishi Lancer, Subaru Impreza, HSV\n- Organizations: PROCAR\n- Events: 2004 Paris Motor Show\n- Topics: Australian Nations Cup Championship, Australian GT Production Car Championship\n\nFacts:\n- In 2000, PROCAR split the Australian GT Production Car Championship into the new Australian Nations Cup Championship for more exotic GT cars and a revised Australian GT Production Car Championship for lesser vehicles.\n- In 2000, Porsche 911s, Ferrari 360s, and Lamborghini Diablos were examples of exotic GT cars that could compete in the Australian Nations Cup Championship.\n- In 2000, Mitsubishi Lancers, Subaru Imprezas, and HSV vehicles were examples of lesser vehicles that could compete for outright wins in the revised Australian GT Production Car Championship.\n- The Ferrari 360 succeeded the Ferrari F355.\n- The Ferrari F430 replaced the Ferrari 360.\n- The Ferrari F430, as the successor to the Ferrari 360, debuted at the 2004 Paris Motor Show.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d9cabfa9-4d26-5b1b-a7f4-ced07d32f477",
        "text": "Page: Ferrari 360. Sentence: It succeeded the Ferrari F355 and was replaced by the Ferrari F430.\nPage: Ferrari F430. Sentence: A successor to the Ferrari 360, it debuted at the 2004 Paris Motor Show.\nPage: Australian Nations Cup Championship. Sentence: For 2000, PROCAR split the Australian GT Production Car Championship into two separate series so that the more exotic GT cars such as Porsche 911s, Ferrari 360s and Lamborghini Diablo's could compete in the new Australian Nations Cup Championship and the lesser vehicles such as the Mitsubishi Lancers, Subaru Imprezas and HSV's could now compete for outright wins in the revised Australian GT Production Car Championship.",
